WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:05.900
When Marc Hayutin was 69, he was diagnosed
with squamous cell thymic carcinoma,

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:09.840
a deadly cancer that affects the lymphatic
and endocrine systems.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.640
His doctor told him he likely had months to
live.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:16.299
One of the most frightening things I've ever
seen was sort of by accident

00:00:16.299 --> 00:00:20.529
when I saw on a medical consultant's laptop
the scan

00:00:20.529 --> 00:00:27.640
metastasized to lungs, liver, thoracic cavity,
my right humerus, my left scapula.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:29.380
That was six years ago.

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:35.120
He survived thanks to an experimental drug
that rapidly shrank his tumors and eased his pain.

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.420
What's particularly noteworthy about Marc
Hayutin's surprising recovery

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:44.080
was that the drug that saved his life hadn't
been approved by the FDA.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:45.960
That would come six years later,

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.960
at which point Hayutin probably would have
been long dead.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:53.040
He was able to take it anyway thanks to Right
to Try—

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:56.469
a legal movement that has led to the passage
of laws in 41 states

00:00:56.469 --> 00:01:00.440
allowing doctors to prescribe terminal patients
with experimental drugs

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:03.140
That haven't passed muster with regulators.

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:06.540
Some bills, they don't have a good name, okay?
they really don't.

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:12.200
But this is such a great name, for the first
day I heard it, it's so perfect 'Right to Try.'

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:17.080
In May of 2019 President Trump signed right
to try into federal law.

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:19.660
It was championed by Vice President Mike Pence

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:23.380
who had signed a version of the legislation
himself in 2015.

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:28.180
Whatever happens next, I've had a great quality
of life, we've continued to travel,

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:31.620
we've been able to take our grandchildren
on trips.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:37.180
Marc Hayutin's story underscores the deadly
consequences of FDA overreach.

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:41.759
The Right to Try laws which were engineered
by a small free market think tank in Arizona

00:01:41.759 --> 00:01:43.620
called The Goldwater Institute,

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:47.320
are a shot across the bow of the FDA's core
mission.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:49.760
It's a real culture change in Washington DC,

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:53.990
it's one of the first times in recent history
where congress has told the FDA

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:55.509
'you're over stepping your boundaries.'

00:01:55.509 --> 00:01:58.680
And I think that that's not the end of the
story, this is just the beginning.

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:03.420
Concern over the tragic effects of the new
sedative thalidomide

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:06.100
prompts president Kennedy at his press conference

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:09.500
to call for stronger, better administered
drug laws.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:12.320
Founded in 1906, the Food and Drug Administration

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:16.820
didn't become a powerful force in american
health care until the 1960s

00:02:16.820 --> 00:02:21.400
when the sedative called thalidomide was marketed
to pregnant women in Europe.

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:23.820
It turned out that when their mothers took
thalidomide

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:28.240
about 40% of babies died in utero or shortly
after birth.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:34.400
Those that survive often had severe birth
defects like shortened limbs and deformed organs.

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:37.620
Americans mostly dodged the thalidomide tragedy

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:41.260
thanks to FDA physician Frances Oldham Kelsey

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:45.440
who was concerned about its safety and kept
it off the US market.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:51.080
Her decision saved thousands of lives and
earned her the presidential medal of freedom.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:52.800
At the time the FDA's mandate

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:57.320
was to determine if a drug had dangerous side
effects before approving it for use.

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:01.360
After the thalidomide scandal a new law gave
the agency broad authority

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:05.060
to determine if new drugs also did what they
claimed

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:10.160
and to prevent americans from trying them
until it's investigation was complete.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:13.840
It turned out there was an enormous cost to
caution.

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:18.920
Thalidomide's hidden victims were those denied
access to life saving medicines

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:21.240
because of regulatory overreach.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:24.760
In the decade after the FDA expanded its scope,

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:28.120
new drug approvals fell by about two thirds.

00:03:28.120 --> 00:03:34.240
Today the average cost to get a drug passed
the FDA to market has risen to 2.7 billion dollars.

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:37.620
And the number of new drugs approved per billion
dollars spent

00:03:37.620 --> 00:03:40.680
has halved every nine years since 1960.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:46.060
And yet there was little public outrage over
the impact on medical innovation.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:52.260
Images of children deformed in utero by an
untested drug spur public action.

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:54.720
But the victims of a regulatory environment

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:59.180
that limits access and curbs innovation go
unnoticed.

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:01.880
But libertarian reformers at the Goldwater
Institute

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:05.460
discovered one scenario that the public could
rally around.

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:11.280
Cases in which terminally ill patients are
denied the right to try experimental drugs.

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:14.580
David Huntley who suffered from ALS,

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:18.980
a neuro-degenerative disease that destroys
all motor function

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:23.960
fought for access to a drug in clinical trials
that could slow the progression of his disease.

00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:29.920
FDA impeded his access to GM604, a potentially
life altering treatment.

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:34.500
Without any treatment I will pas in 3 to 9
months.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:39.900
How is keeping a potentially life saving drug
from me saving my life?

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:44.040
The decision to use an investigational drug
to fight a fatal disease

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:47.400
should be between a patient and his or her
doctor.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:51.820
Huntley died in 2015 and never got access
to GM604

00:04:51.820 --> 00:04:55.640
which is in limbo after completing phase 2
clinical trials.

00:04:55.640 --> 00:05:00.020
In September 2016 ALS sufferers testified
before Congress.

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:04.640
Right now we have no hope, so it's either
hope or no hope.

00:05:04.640 --> 00:05:09.100
And this bill, it doesn't do everything,

00:05:09.100 --> 00:05:11.860
but it moves us in the direction of having a little bit of hope.

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:16.820
When chemotherapy failed to shrink Marc Hayutin's
tumors

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:19.820
there were no FDA approved treatment options
left.

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:24.900
But he was accepted into a clinical trial
run by Dr Ebrahim Delpassand

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:31.560
a nuclear oncologist testing an experimental
drug call lutetium-177 Dotatate.

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:34.580
It turned out to have very few side effects.

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:36.900
Many of our patients, if you look at them,

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:40.480
you can't even tell that they are undergoing
cancer therapy.

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:43.840
So typically they don't lose their hair, they
don't get sick.

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:47.000
My cough disappeared, the tumors started to
shrink

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:49.260
and people literally thought I was cured.

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:52.220
Nobody knew why it was working, but it was
working.

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:54.760
At the end of the final phase 3 clinical trial

00:05:54.760 --> 00:05:58.280
the drug had eliminated tumors in a majority
of patients.

00:05:58.280 --> 00:06:03.240
After the trial concluded two and a half years
went by before the FDA would approve the drug.

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:06.480
In the meantime Dr Delpassand requested permission
from the agency

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:09.740
to continue administering it to participants
like Hayutin

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:11.520
who were being kept alive by the treatment.

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:14.000
and to new patients with endocrine cancer.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:15.940
Many cancer patients they can't wait.

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:19.840
The disease in an advanced phase and if we
lose that momentum,

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:24.540
if we do not give them the treatment on time,

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:28.640
essentially we lose that opportunity to control
the disease.

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:30.680
His request was denied.

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:33.160
It was a very difficult time for me at that
point

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:37.580
because what can I tell these patients and
their family members?

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:40.620
Hayutin asked the FDA to reconsider.

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:42.540
The FDA wrote a nice letter to me saying

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:46.520
'well thanks very much but we can see you
didn't in fact have a complete response.'

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:47.920
and I said 'well maybe not,

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:53.020
but I would have been dead before I was writing
this letter without the response I did have

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:57.640
and only a few cancers have the possibility
so far of complete responses.'

00:06:57.640 --> 00:07:00.850
The odds are already so stacked against the
patient

00:07:00.850 --> 00:07:05.320
it doesn't help to have the FDA taking positions
like this.

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:07.520
The FDA should never be the decision maker.

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:13.160
The FDA can require testing, the FDA can make
sure that people have enough information

00:07:13.160 --> 00:07:15.080
so that they can make good decisions.

00:07:15.080 --> 00:07:18.160
But at the end of the day it should be the
patient and the doctor

00:07:18.169 --> 00:07:20.270
who are the ones that are making the decision.

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:22.410
The Goldwater Institute presented the problems

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:26.460
that Dr Delpassand's patients were having
to the Texas legislature.

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:29.320
Which quickly passed a Right to Try law.

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:35.580
Hayutin got continued access to the treatment
as did 77 of Delpassand's other patients.

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:42.140
In the roughly two years between when the
FDA told him to stop giving these treatments to patients

00:07:42.140 --> 00:07:47.640
to when the treatment was finally approved
for market, he treated about 200 patients.

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:52.340
And many of them were told they only had months
to live and many of them are still alive today.

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:55.060
Though the FDA declined our interview requests,

00:07:55.070 --> 00:08:00.350
the agency has emphasized it's own expanded
access program called compassionate use

00:08:00.350 --> 00:08:03.990
which allows some terminal patients to try
experimental drugs.

00:08:03.990 --> 00:08:07.419
According to a 2017 government accountability
office report

00:08:07.419 --> 00:08:12.980
the FDA approved 5697 of 5753 requests

00:08:12.980 --> 00:08:17.669
that came through the compassionate use program
between 2012 and 2015.

00:08:17.669 --> 00:08:22.160
Critics argue that because of this program
Right to Try laws are superfluous.

00:08:22.160 --> 00:08:25.160
But before Right to Try getting approval through
compassionate use

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:28.000
involved significant bureaucratic hurdles.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:30.740
According tot he nonprofit Regulatory Affairs

00:08:30.740 --> 00:08:35.680
Right to Try state laws pressured the FDA
to streamline the application process.

00:08:35.680 --> 00:08:41.820
Delpassand used the FDA's expanded access
program to provide some of his patients with the drug

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:44.740
but once the drug was on track for commercial
approval,

00:08:44.740 --> 00:08:48.160
the FDA wouldn't let him enroll any new patients.

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:50.900
It was only through Texas' right to try law

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:54.400
that the rest of Delpassand's patients, including
Hayutin,

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:56.740
were able to continue to get their treatment.

00:08:56.740 --> 00:08:58.980
I wouldn't be sitting here talking to you
now

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:02.020
if compassionate use was all that I had.

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:07.400
Right to Try allows pharmaceutical companies
to sell experimental drugs at cost

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:12.380
and unlike compassionate use, protects them
from liability and reporting requirements

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:15.920
if a patient dies or suffers on account of
a bad reaction.

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:20.560
No pharmaceutical in their right mind would
give me a drug compassionately

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:24.650
because if I have an adverse event, which,
you know, I'm an ALS patient

00:09:24.650 --> 00:09:29.300
let's face it, I probably will, they have
to report that to the FDA

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:31.620
and it's gonna jeopardize their trial.

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:37.140
Drug approvals have been derailed in the past
by adverse effects occurring under compassionate use.

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:41.580
A patient should be able to weigh the potential
that the drug might now work

00:09:41.580 --> 00:09:44.000
or the drug might have very serious side effects

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:47.760
against the potential that the drug could
actually save their lives.

00:09:47.760 --> 00:09:50.400
Nobody can make that decision for a patient.

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:53.340
That is up to the individual themselves.

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:57.320
Not a drug company, not a doctor and certainly
not the FDA.

00:09:57.320 --> 00:10:02.520
Critics also say Right to Try has only helped
a small number of patients so far.

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:05.160
Although there are no official numbers to
date.

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:07.610
Sandefur says that she expects that to change

00:10:07.610 --> 00:10:11.980
as drug manufacturers and doctors become more
familiar with the law.

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:16.840
Right to Try is a very small but a very important
first step in the right direction.

00:10:16.850 --> 00:10:20.540
Ultimately I would love to see a system that
is more patient centric.

00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:23.540
I would love to see a system that gives patients
more options

00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:25.980
so that they don't have to be beholden to
the FDA.

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:28.140
And that's especially true of terminally ill
patients

00:10:28.149 --> 00:10:30.990
because when people are fighting for their
lives

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:33.180
they shouldn't have to fight the government
as well.

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:36.520
Sandefur says that the Right to Try

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:40.820
is just the beginning of the push for greater
pharmaceutical freedom.

00:10:40.820 --> 00:10:43.320
Policy researcher Bartley Madden for example

00:10:43.320 --> 00:10:47.380
has floated a proposal, already being implemented
in Japan,

00:10:47.380 --> 00:10:52.640
under which any patient could access experimental
drugs that pass initial safety screenings

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:54.780
without any FDA involvement at all.

00:10:54.780 --> 00:10:58.540
Their outcomes would be logged in publicly
accessible databases

00:10:58.540 --> 00:11:02.960
enabling a freer flow of information and possibly
faster breakthroughs.

00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:08.760
Thanks to Right to Try, Marc Hayutin has lived
for six years

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:11.740
beyond his doctor's initial expectations.

00:11:11.740 --> 00:11:17.100
Time that he's spent with his wife of 54 years,
vacationing with his grandchildren

00:11:17.110 --> 00:11:19.370
and caring for his elderly mother.

00:11:19.370 --> 00:11:24.540
But recently his cancer returned and he's
now searching for another miracle.

00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:27.680
I don't know how soon this journey will end

00:11:27.680 --> 00:11:31.760
but I am really grateful for Right to Try.

